 "transcript": {
  "answer": "The Aussie has risen against both currencies since the start of 2026, though it has pulled back from its September highs.\n\n**Against the US dollar (AUD/USD):**\n- End of December 2025: US$0.6693\n- Latest (16 October 2026): US$0.6947\n- That is a gain of about 3.8%, or roughly 2.5 US cents.\n- The path was choppy. It climbed to about US$0.7126 in February, dipped to US$0.6845 at end-March, then peaked near US$0.7166 at end-August. On 9 September it touched US$0.7232, the high of the daily data. It has since slipped about 3% from that level.\n\n**Against the euro (AUD/EUR):**\n- End of December 2025: €0.5704\n- Latest (16 October 2026): €0.6187\n- That is a gain of about 8.5%, a larger move than against the US dollar. This implies the euro weakened against the US dollar over the period.\n- The rise was steady. It reached about €0.6154 at end-May, dipped to €0.6034 in June, and has held around €0.61–0.62 since July.\n\nMonth-end figures are used for the 2026 path and the December 2025 starting point. The latest figures are the 16 October daily rates from the RBA.",
